# Codename and attack rules

These rules apply to every character. Codenames identify animations, attacks and attack-created objects consistently across character definitions, assets, combat, controls, AI and UI metadata. A character's mechanics and player-facing move names remain character-specific; the internal naming system is shared.

## Character IDs and animation filenames

Each character has a four-digit ID, stored as a string with leading zeroes. Use the character's own ID wherever <id> appears below.

The standard asset path is:

assets/characters/<id>/<id>_<animation>_<frame>.png

Use lowercase codenames and underscores. Numbered frames start at 1 and play in numeric order. The final number identifies the animation frame, never the attack number or an object instance. A single-frame animation still ends in _1.

| Animation | Filename pattern |
| --- | --- |
| Idle | <id>_idle_<frame>.png |
| Run | <id>_run_<frame>.png |
| Jump | <id>_jump_<frame>.png |
| Fall | <id>_fall_<frame>.png |
| Land | <id>_land_<frame>.png |
| Hurt | <id>_hurt_<frame>.png |
| Mid-air hurt | <id>_midair_hurt_<frame>.png |
| Shield preparation | <id>_prepshield_<frame>.png |
| Shield hold | <id>_shielding_<frame>.png |
| Shield release | <id>_releaseshield_<frame>.png |
| Mid-air shield | <id>_midair_shielding_<frame>.png |
| Movement / dash | <id>_mouvment_<frame>.png |
| Mid-air movement | <id>_midair_mouvment_<frame>.png |
| Numbered attack | <id>_attackN_<frame>.png |
| Mid-air numbered attack | <id>_midair_attackN_<frame>.png |
| Extra attack | <id>_extra_attack_<frame>.png |
| Charge startup | <id>_charge_<frame>.png |
| Charge loop | <id>_charge_a.png and <id>_charge_b.png |

Replace N with the attack number, from 1 through 5. Preserve the codename spelling mouvment for animation keys and assets, including midair_mouvment.

Charge startup frames play once in order. After startup, charge_a and charge_b loop while Charge is held. These lettered frames are the explicit exception to numbered animation frames.

## Numbered attacks and airborne versions

A character has at least 2 and at most 5 numbered attack families: attack1 through attack5. Numbers must be sequential, with no gaps. Ground and mid-air versions belong to the same attack family, and Charge replacements count toward the numbered total.

Every independently accessible numbered attack must have a functional midair_attackN definition and animation. An attack that exists only as a Charge replacement is exempt from this requirement. An independent attack5 always requires midair_attack5.

Attack numbers identify moves; they do not prescribe damage, effects or mechanics. A numbered attack may be a direct strike, projectile, summon, deployable or another character-specific ability.

extra_attack is a separate, additional character-specific action. It may be a projectile, throw, utility move or other unique attack. It does not count toward the 2–5 numbered attack limit, so a character may have five numbered attacks plus extra_attack. Its airborne availability is defined by the character's loadout.

Shield, movement, jump, Charge and transform are not numbered attacks.

## Charge and attack-button routing

charge identifies the held stance that changes which attack an existing button triggers. It is not a separate family of charged attack names.

Charge availability is a character capability. A character may have Charge without having any replacement attacks. Characters without Charge use their numbered attacks as independent buttons.

| Numbered attack count | Charge available | Independent numbered buttons | Replacements while charging |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 2 | No | attack1, attack2 | None |
| 3 | No | attack1, attack2, attack3 | None |
| 4 | No | attack1, attack2, attack3, attack4 | None |
| 5 | No | attack1, attack2, attack3, attack4, attack5 | None |
| 2 | Yes | attack1, attack2 | None |
| 3 | Yes | attack1, attack2 | attack1 → attack3 |
| 4 | Yes | attack1, attack2 | attack1 → attack3; attack2 → attack4 |
| 5 | Yes | attack1, attack2, attack5 | attack1 → attack3; attack2 → attack4 |

For a charged five-attack loadout, attack5 remains independent while charging. Its button always identifies attack5.

Charge-exclusive replacements use the existing base attack buttons and do not get duplicate independent buttons. Whether attack3 or attack4 is an independent move or a Charge replacement depends on the loadout, not its codename.

For example, attack3 on a character without Charge is an independent third attack with midair_attack3. On a charged loadout, attack3 can instead replace attack1 and need no independent button or mid-air version.

Controls, AI, UI labels, cooldowns and combat must agree on the same loadout and routing. A character without Charge must not expose an unusable Charge action or enter the Charge stance.

## Mid-air shield and dodge fallback

Characters with mid-air shield frames use midair_shielding for their airborne shield.

Characters with no mid-air shield frames use their midair_mouvment animation frames to dodge in mid-air instead of performing a mid-air shield. This fallback is a dodge action, not a shield displayed with different art.

Each mid-air dodge costs 25 energy. The character must have at least 25 energy to begin the dodge, and the cost is deducted once when the dodge starts.

## Attack-created objects

Projectiles, clones, summons, deployables and attack-owned effects use the codename of the attack that creates them.

The standard object filename is:

<id>_<attackCodename>_object_<frame>.png

| Owning attack | Object filename pattern |
| --- | --- |
| attackN | <id>_attackN_object_<frame>.png |
| midair_attackN | <id>_midair_attackN_object_<frame>.png |
| extra_attack | <id>_extra_attack_object_<frame>.png |

The object codename records gameplay ownership rather than a fictional technique or display name. Runtime instance IDs may distinguish concurrent objects, but must not change the asset frame suffix.

If an attack creates multiple visually distinct object types, use documented child keys beneath its object namespace, such as attackN_object_<type>_<frame>. Do not use frame numbers to distinguish object types.

A summon may explicitly reuse its owner's animation clips and hit data. Shared art does not need duplicate PNGs; summon-owned art and effects still follow the creating attack's object namespace.

## Internal codenames and display names

Use attack1 through attack5, midair_attack1 through midair_attack5, extra_attack and charge as the shared internal vocabulary. Do not introduce alternate internal move names based on a character, technique or player-facing label.

Legacy terms such as ba, maba, cba and uniqueba are not live attack IDs. They may appear only when documenting history or reading older saved data for migration.

Player-facing names such as “Punch,” “Kick” or other character-specific move names may appear in menus and descriptions. They do not replace canonical IDs in character definitions, animation keys, filenames, controls, AI, cooldowns or object references.

When adding or updating a character, keep its attack count, Charge routing, airborne variants, object ownership and animation assets consistent with these shared rules.
